### Scientific interpretation

The spectral characterization becomes progressively closer
to the canonical reference as the subsample size increases.

The effective dimensionality is comparatively stable, while
variance-retention dimensions and eigenspace alignment show
greater sampling variation at N=500 and substantially greater
stability at N≥1500.

This supports the interpretation that the broad spectral
structure is not dependent on one particular sampling subset.

---

### Permitted manuscript claim

> "The spectral structure of the morphology representation was
> stable under repeated uniform subsampling, with effective
> dimensionality remaining close to the canonical estimate and
> variance-retention dimensions converging toward the full-sample
> values with increasing sample size."

### Stronger wording NOT justified

Do NOT write:

> "The morphology spectrum is invariant to sampling."

The results show variation, particularly at N=500.

Do NOT write:

> "The exact intrinsic dimensionality is 73."

D95=73 is a variance-retention criterion, not a mathematical
intrinsic dimension.

Do NOT write:

> "The morphology has an intrinsic dimension of 10.55."

10.55 is a participation-ratio effective dimension.

---

### Limitations

The resampling procedure tests sensitivity to observation
composition within the available dataset.

It does not establish robustness to:

- new datasets
- different sketch populations
- different acquisition protocols
- different morphology representations

---

### Disposition

**CORE — supporting robustness evidence**

This is useful because it strengthens the credibility of the
canonical morphology spectrum, but it should not become the
central scientific result.

