---

### Primary findings

#### 1. Global graph connectivity

All 2300 observations belong to a single connected component.

This indicates that the k=10 morphology neighborhood graph does
not fragment into disconnected geometric islands.

#### 2. Local traversability

Local geodesic distances are approximately 1.87× the corresponding
Euclidean distances at the measured local scale.

Thus graph paths are longer than direct Euclidean distances, but
the graph remains locally traversable.

#### 3. Distance-order preservation

Euclidean and graph-geodesic distances show strong rank agreement:

median Spearman rho = 0.903.

This indicates that observations that are relatively near or far
under Euclidean morphology geometry tend to retain similar ordering
under graph-geodesic distance.

---

### Scientific interpretation

The combined evidence supports the following restricted claim:

> The morphology neighborhood graph forms a connected,
> locally traversable geometric structure in which
> graph-geodesic relationships remain strongly related
> to the underlying Euclidean morphology geometry.

This is evidence for **organized morphology geometry**.

It is NOT evidence that the graph itself represents semantic
morphology categories.

---

### Relationship to E04

E04 established:

    smooth local neighborhood growth

E05 establishes:

    connected local-neighborhood traversability

Together:

    local continuity
          +
    graph connectivity
          +
    Euclidean–geodesic rank agreement

provide convergent evidence that the morphology representation
forms an organized geometric space rather than a collection of
isolated observations.

---

### Important qualification

The graph parameters matter.

This result was obtained for:

    k = 10

Therefore the manuscript should not state that morphology is
globally connected under every possible neighborhood definition.

The appropriate claim is:

> "At k=10, the morphology neighborhood graph was fully
> connected..."

A robustness analysis over several k values would strengthen this
claim if required.

---

### Geodesic stretch interpretation

The median stretch of approximately 1.90 indicates that graph
paths are substantially longer than direct Euclidean distances.

Therefore we should NOT describe the morphology space as
"nearly Euclidean."

Instead:

> "Geodesic distances remained strongly rank-consistent with
> Euclidean morphology distances despite moderate path stretch."

This is the scientifically safer statement.

---

